Id: aud012w
Severity: WARN
Category: logging
Check: check_audit
Fix: chown root:log_group and chmod 0640 (0600 without a log_group) the audit log; start auditd if the file is missing.
References: auditd.conf(5); auditd(8); CIS Distribution Independent Linux Benchmark 4.1.4 (Configure auditd file access)
Controls: CIS 8.2, 8.5; NIST AU-2, AU-12; ISO 8.15

auditd is configured on this Linux host (running; or, on an offline
root, installed and enabled at boot) and its log file, log_file in
/etc/audit/auditd.conf (/var/log/audit/audit.log by default), is
missing, or is not root's, or can be read or written by someone other
than root and the group log_group names. auditd creates the file 0600,
or 0640 owned by root and log_group when log_group is set
(auditd.conf(5)), and the CIS Benchmarks ask for 0640 or tighter. The
audit trail records who logged in, who used privileges and who touched
the watched files: readable by anyone, it tells every local account
what the others did; writable, it can be edited by whoever it
incriminates; missing, nothing auditd records is kept.

Fedora and RHEL, which install auditd by default, ship it with the file
root:root 0600, so a stock host says nothing. Fix the mode and owner
('chown root:adm /var/log/audit/audit.log; chmod 0640 ...' where
log_group is adm, 0600 otherwise); a missing file comes back when
auditd is restarted, and if /var/log/audit itself is gone, recreate it
root's and mode 0700 first.
